{"agent":"impact","findings":[{"id":"impact-001","file":"src/angular/src/app/pages/main/notification-bell.component.html","line":28,"title":"Notification text switched from escaped interpolation to [innerHTML], allowing HTML injection from server and remote error strings","category":"blast-radius","cwe":"CWE-79","severity":"high","agent_confidence":80,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The old `{{ notif.text }}` escaped all markup in every case. The new `[innerHTML]` parses the text as HTML, and the only thing standing between it and the DOM is Angular's default DomSanitizer. Several producers pass strings the backend or remote side controls straight into `text`: `status.server.errorMessage` (header.component.ts:49), `latestRemoteScanError` through STATUS_REMOTE_SERVER_ERROR (header.component.ts:96, localization.ts:15-16), the bulk `result.errorMessage` through Bulk.ERROR (file-list.component.ts:554, localization.ts:74-75), and settings `reaction.errorMessage` (settings-page.component.ts:170,239,257). Any of these can echo file names, remote shell output or config values. The sanitizer removes script tags and on* handlers but keeps `&lt;a href&gt;` and `&lt;img src&gt;`. So a remote file name or scan error like `&lt;a href=\"https://evil.example\"&gt;Click to re-authenticate&lt;/a&gt;` or `&lt;img src=\"https://tracker.example/x\"&gt;` now renders as a working phishing link or a beacon that fetches from outside, inside a trusted DANGER notification.","current_code":"&lt;span class=\"bell-notif-text\" [innerHTML]=\"notif.text\"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;","fix_hint":"Revert to `{{ notif.text }}`. If one producer (e.g. the version URL) really needs a link, add a typed structured field such as `linkUrl` instead of treating all text as HTML","why_it_matters":"A protection that held for every input has been swapped for one that depends on a library default sanitizer, and it now covers every notification producer, including ones that relay remote, attacker-influenced